    "body": "Lockheed Martin is investing $300 million to expand munitions manufacturing in Grand Prairie, Texas , part of a larger production push that the company says will create 6,000 jobs nationwide, including 2,000 in Texas.\nWhat’s Related\nThe expansion will bring the Grand Prairie operation’s manufacturing space to more than 735,000 square feet. It includes 100,000 square feet of new space and converting 85,000 square feet of offices into manufacturing space.\nLockheed Martin also announced that manufacturing company Hadrian will install 24 automated machining machines at the facility. Lockheed Martin production employees will operate the equipment, which is expected to begin running in early 2027.\nThe site makes components for the company’s missile programs and supplies final-assembly operations in locations including Camden, Arkansas, and Troy, Alabama. The expansion adds room for automation and equipment that makes parts to precise measurements.\nRELATED STORIES: Amazon Plans Multibillion-Dollar Robotics Plant in Austin | Nvidia and Corning Open New AI Factories in Texas and North Carolina\nConverting offices into manufacturing areas\nSome of that work is already underway. Lockheed Martin said it has converted former offices into manufacturing areas and installed more than 30 additional precision machines in recent months, with that equipment now producing thousands of components each week.\nBy the end of 2026, the company expects to have added nearly 100 advanced machines throughout the facility. Nearly 800 production employees currently work there, and the operation manufactured 250,000 parts in 2025.\n“Lockheed Martin has brought together commercial speed and combat-proven performance because America needs both,” said Jim Taiclet, the company’s chairman, president and CEO.\nHadrian joins the party\nHadrian’s separate announcement describes a seven-year agreement that builds on a memorandum of understanding announced in December 2025. It brings the company’s automated manufacturing equipment, Opus software , and workforce training into Lockheed Martin’s Grand Prairie operation.\nThe companies plan to phase in additional groups of machines over the agreement, with each group serving multiple missile programs. Hadrian said the arrangement also allows production to shift among its other factories as demand changes.\nThe approach puts more component machining inside Lockheed Martin’s own facility while retaining access to Hadrian’s outside factories. Hadrian operates four facilities in California, Arizona, and Alabama.\n“The warfighter can't wait on peacetime timelines,” said Chris Power, Hadrian’s CEO.\nPart of a larger factory investment\nThe Grand Prairie project is part of Lockheed Martin’s planned $8 billion to $9 billion investment through 2030 to increase munitions production. That spending includes modernizing or expanding more than 20 U.S. facilities.\nLockheed Martin said agreements covering several missile programs give it greater predictability when investing in employees, equipment, and suppliers.",
